ething you'll be hearing some talks  about later today you  to do something more than just observe  you have to actually intervene you have  to run experiments and doing macroscale  experiments on social systems systems  that involve people is not only more  difficult it's often impossible or  unethical and so you have these you have  these tremendous constraints on what you  can do and so this makes social science  just a different kind of exercise from  physical science or biological science  where you can you can kind of set up  labs and you can study things under  controlled circumstances and so if you  think about the scientific method where  you generate your hypothesis and then  you design your experiment and you go  and measure things and then you test  your hypothesis you you sort of can't do  the second two-thirds of this much of  the time and so if you think about  what's happened over the last hundred  years social science is generated a  tremendous number of interesting  theories about the world but our ability  to test these theories and decide  between them  has been extremely limited so the  exciting thing about what's happened in  the last ten or fifteen years is that  the the technological revolution of the  web and the Internet and digital  communication and other related  technologies is starting to lift some of  these long-standing barriers and so  there's two specific ways in which this  is happening the first is the sort of  the Big Data revolution where people are  going about their ordinary business  they're communicating with each other  they're they're participating in social  media they're talking on the phone  they're sending email they're they're  collaborating and in the process they're  there they're engaging in e-commerce and  in the process they're spewing off this  enormous amount of data that is that is  you know by good fortune interesting to  social scientists so we have a this kind  of passive data collection exercise just  happening in real time and we can  benefit from all of this data to ask  interesting questions or to ask the same  questions that we've been asking all  along but we can we can  new data to bear on them and the second  thing that's happening is that we can  also use the web to to run experiments  in ways that are quite distinct from  what we've been able to do in the past  or they're analogous to that but they're  they're distinct in terms of speed and  scale so you know one thing that I'll  talk about this morning is is how we've  taken this traditional model of the lab  experiment that you're probably all  familiar with from from college you  probably either saw these things or  participated in them where there's some  sort of lab experiment happening in the  psychology department so there's a  version of that that can be done online  now and also field experiments which  perhaps soon on our I'll will talk about  today and so if you kind of lump all of  this together in a very loose sense it's  this is what we really refer to as  computational social science it's  intersection of questions that come from  the social sciences and methods that  come from the computational sciences and  so this is a very active area of  research right now and there's a lot of  excitement in this area we just ran the  second international conference on  computational social science this this  past June in Chicago and there's another  one happening in Germany next year so  this is an area that's really getting  off the ground so just to be a bit more  concrete I want to spend the rest of the  talk really just discussing two examples  of what I mean what I mean by  computational social science that  illustrate these two types of data  collections so the first one is using  passively collected data from online  sources to study this very old question  about social contagion how things spread  and the second one is using a highly  designed system where we set up a live  experiment that we have designed  ourselves and we recruit people into  paid to understand another old question  about how people solve problems in teams  and so what's you know a couple of  things that I find interesting about  this work is you know first that we're  all of it is motivated by these  long-standing questions that we have in  social science going back decades or  possibly even longer but we are also  driving using these questions to drive  kind of new methodologies that allow us  to shed new light on these questions and  then finally if I have time we can also  discuss you know what this work shows us  is problematic that you know we're able  to do lots of interesting things but in  the course of doing these things we also  realize that we have all these new  problems that we didn't that we didn't  necessarily have before and that  hopefully charts out a path for the  future of computational social science  so I'll just jump right in start talking  about the first example so when you  think about contagion  you probably have a mental model that  looks something like the following and  this is a model that underlies  essentially all of the formal  mathematical models that have been  proposed in the literature all of which  or essentially come from a notion of  biological contagion so when we the  words that we use when we talk about  things going viral or things spreading  or diffusing throughout a population  come from this biological metaphor in  which this mental model is pretty  accurate right so if you think about the  Ebola epidemic that that started in in  West Africa a couple of years ago it  really was the case that you know a  single person and index person like that  red dot and middle there was infected by  the Ebola virus we're jumping across  from some animal reservoir and then that  person went home and infected a small  number of his or her family members or  neighbors and each one  them got sick and infected a few of  their neighbors and each one of them got  sick and infected if you were their  neighbors in over the course of several  months you see thousands of people  getting infected and through this  multi-step branching process so you sort  of imagine you know at every stage is  only a few people are getting infected  by each infected person but this is  happening over and over again and you  you're getting exponential growth and so  when we talk about things going viral in  the social sphere we have the same  mental model we assume that something  like this is happening in step rather  than a disease being passed along  it's an idea or a piece of information  or a behavioral trait and in fact most  of the models that we that we use to to  describe social contagion are based on  on this assumption as well so the  problem is that when we measure things  spreading in the social world we don't  actually have data that looks like that  mental model typically what we have or  historically what we've had is data that  looks like this where you have some kind  of aggregate measure of the total number  of people who have adopted a product or  who have retweeted a tweet or have  clicked on a YouTube video and you can  measure that over time and so typically  what you get is a curve that looks like  an s-shaped curve where you see some you  know early period where a few people are  paying attention to it and then you see  this exponential growth period and then  you see some kind of asymptote where the  susceptible population gets exhausted  and and the epidemic dies out and so the  the assumption is that whenever you see  an s-shaped curve there's some contagion  process which is happening underneath  because if you simulate one of these  models you get something that looks like  that the problem empirically is that  that you can get s-shaped curves in lots  of other ways as well so just because  you're a model generates an escape shape  curve and just because you see an  s-shaped curve in the wild doesn't  mean that yo