he course of 12  months more or less everything that  contained a link to a video or a news  story or an image for a petition that  was posted to Twitter for a 12-month  period so if anything is gonna go viral  we're going to see it and then the  second thing we did is we threw out  everything that you could sort of  you wasn't intended to be popular right  so one of the critiques of these one of  the tricky parts of doing this kind of  study is like clearly a lot of stuff  that people post to Twitter it's never  supposed to spread right they just you  know they're just posting it because  they're interested in or because they  want to show it to a family member or  something so you could argue that it's  sort of unfair to count everything so we  just throw out everything that isn't  plausibly popular and so in fact we we  have a very high threshold for that we  we only look at things that have at  least a hundred retweets so these things  are way more popular than anything that  I just showed you on the previous slide  so this is like one in three thousand  events qualifies as being in our data  set so we're looking at extremely rare  events but because we have over a  billion of them to start with we still  get three hundred and fifty thousand of  these rare event so this is where the  the big part of the Big Data starts to  help you is that when you're studying  rare events and you need to have a lot  of them you need to have an enormous  population to begin with okay so for  each one of these cascades which are at  least a hundred retweets in size we can  study this property that we define  called structural virality and so what  we mean by it is that if you look at one  of these if you let something diffuse so  it goes through its whole process of  cascading and at the end of it you have  some trace of where it's been right so  this is the pattern that you see and now  you have this structure you can compute  properties of it and so the property  that we're gonna compute is a fairly  simple one which is just the average  path length so how many steps does it  take to get from anyone to anyone else  in this structure which we're just going  to treat as a network or a graph  whatever we want to call it so something  that is a broadcast know where somebody  introduces something and a lot of people  retweet it and then nothing else happens  that's going to have an average path  length of close to two all right so the  the smallest structural virality you can  have is two and then if you get one of  these very large multi-step branching  processes the depth of that tree is  going to be proportional to the log of  the number of nodes in the tree and so  the average path length is also going to  be portion  to login so you for every one of these  structures you get some number that's  somewhere between two and log n and so  now you can compute that number for all  350,000 of these events and you can  start asking empirical questions about  them so you know how much diversity do  you expect to see for example so if you  look at big events do they tend to be  very viral in this structural sense lots  and lots of generations of branching  what do they tend to be very  broadcasting right and then you can also  ask well as you look at things of  different sizes do they become more  viral or more broadcasted and so you can  sort of imagine in your make a private  prediction I won't make anybody put  their hands up but you can you know  imagine like well what do you think is  like the optimal way to spread like what  kind of structure is going to generate  the the most the most spreading is it  going to be a broadcast is it going to  be a viral thing is it going to be some  kind of optimal combination of the two  and so you can come up with all kinds of  stories about what you imagine will work  better and the first answer is that  whatever story you came up with you're  right right that there's something in  there that looks like what you're  imagining there is sort of so the first  lesson here is that there is tremendous  diversity in this sort of huge  population of things that are spreading  we find almost everything right and so  if we rank so the so the first thing to  note about these pictures is they all  have the about the same number of nodes  in them so everything in here is roughly  as popular as everything else right but  the structure is wildly different so the  thing up on the top left is that that  looks like a big black triangle with  some paint dripping off it like that is  a huge broadcast node so there's a CNN  story and so CNN has lots of followers  so they post a news story and a whole  bunch of people retweet it and then  basically nothing much else happens  right all the way down on the on the  bottom right is a was a sort of  remarkable obit in the New York Times  about this this world war 2 hero  who had led this sort of incredibly  interesting life and it was not somebody  who anyone heard of and so it wasn't a  famous person but people found this very  interesting and so it got a lot of  retweets but the structure of these  retweets was totally different that you  have many many generations where just a  few people at each generation are  retweeting the previous tweet so so the  first lesson here is you have tremendous  diversity in structure the second lesson  is if you look at these little inserts  underneath the the figures that's  showing you what you would have seen  sort of in the old world where you just  got the number to count with respect to  time so this is the the non structural  data and you can see that almost all of  these things look the same there's no  way you could rank these things properly  if you just saw popularity over time the  structure is really telling us something  different about about the about the the  nature of the spreading thing and so  this is a metric which is now going into  [Music]  Bing  where we can we can use this as another  way to rank popular events and and the  hope is that that we uncover some other  feature of interestingness that is  different from just flat-out popularity  so the second question you know how do  things change with size is also somewhat  surprising but so this is now is  breaking all these events up by domain  and plotting the structural virality by  popularity so each one of these bins  contains tweets of or Cascades of  roughly the same size and the box plot  shows you the inter quartile range of  the of the structural virality and the  black bars show you the median and so  it's a couple of interesting things here  the for pictures and videos we see  almost no correlation between  structural violence eyes so big things  are no more viral and this structural  sense than small things and the reason  is that on Twitter you have you know the  these incredibly connected individuals  like the who the most popular person is  anymore I think Katy Perry overtook  Justin Bieber last year but they have  you know eighty million followers so I  mean there's sort of enormous ly  connected individuals and if one of them  gets involved then they can really  generate a huge number of retweets and  so essentially what's happening here is  that the the size the the the the size  of the entire structure is driven by the  size of the largest broadcast in that  structure and so this is what we used to  call us the Justin Bieber effect but I  guess it's now the Katy Perry effect or  whatever and it turns out that you know  celebrity so almost all the most follow  people on Twitter are celebrities and  celebrities are exclusively interested  in themselves so they don't tweet about  the news right  they just tweet pictures and videos so  if you look at pictures and videos there  it's highly celebrity driven and so you  see no correlation with news you see  more of a correlation petitions are also  interesting because they are just more  viral on average right so I think this  sort of you're sort of seeing  interesting level domain effects here  but just to sum up I think that of the  theoretical lesson here and this is  something that's you know interesting to  me in particular because I've been  involved in this theoretical literature  for you know 20 years now and there's  thousands of papers written about models  of diffusion and really they're all  concerned with this notion of the  threshold where you know that when you  get above a certain critical level of of  infectiousness you go from things dying  out to things  spreading exponentially and if you're an  epidemiologist you want to get  everything below this critical threshold  cuz you don't want things to spray  if your social marketing person you want  to get everything above the threshold  because you want your thing to go viral  but everyone is sort of worried about  this tipping point and what we find is  that this is a very misleading analogy  right that everything we see is below  this critical threshold everything is  dying out all the time and the only  thing that is driving it are these big  hubs these these large seeds so we have  to sort of think differently about how  things spread in a social environment  then how they spread in a biological  environment and so we need to kind of  break this historical analogy that we've  been relying on for our models and of  course you know once you understand that  then is that you can you can also think  differently about how you're going to to  apply this in practice and finally I  think you know again in in in in my  world there's sort of a lot of criticism  of Big Data because it has all kinds of  problems that I'm sure you you know  about but this is really an example  where the size of the data set is really  helpful but if you're looking for rare  events and and you know it's number of  places in in social science where we do  in fact care about rare events it's very  difficult to study rare events and this  is so having a large data set like this  really enables you to do things that  historically you could not do okay so  okay so I want to talk about the second  problem now which is using a different  type of data generating mechanism a lab  environment to study problem solving how  many few minutes oh okay all right let  me just try to sum up so we're  interested in how groups of people solve  problems  just trust me people have been thinking  about this for a long time we have no  idea we would like to do this in an  experimental setting and so as I  mentioned earlier you know we can now  take this historical model of the lab  which you see on the you know in the  sort of grainy black-and-white picture  here and we can do it online using sites  like Amazon's Mechanical Turk and so the  problem that that we studied is a  problem that you may have heard of  it's called crisis mapping this is a  real thing that people do in the world  when there's a crisis happening  somewhere like a natural disaster like  in Haiti the earthquake in 2010  volunteers assemble online and they what  they do is they monitor all the social  media activity of users on the ground  and they classify all this noisy  unstructured data to find where things  are damaged where people are needing  help and they can then hand this cleaned  up structured data set in the form of a  map and crisis map to an agency like the  UN which is then uses the map to direct  resources so this is a useful thing it's  a complex task  it's a real-world task and what we're  interested in is is using this task as a  way to understand how people collaborate  in a controlled environment and so this  is a virtual lab experiment using  software that was developed by one of  our postdocs Andrew mal so this runs on  meteor and uses some software that he  wrote called tooks over  which recruits people from Amazon  Mechanical Turk and then brings them  into this experimental setting and what  they're doing here is they're we give  them about 1,500 tweets which are real  tweets from Twitter users in the  Philippines in December 2012 when the  Philippines was hit by a typhoon typhoon  Pablo and so you can see what what  happened is this typhoon swept through  the center of the Philippines there and  people are tweeting about it and most of  these tweets are not very informative  but some of them contain very concrete  information about damage on the ground  and so the objective is that these group  of stalkers have to find the relevant  tweets extract the relevant information  from those tweets classify these events  in terms of is it damaged housing is it  displaced population are the deaths  reported what is the description and  then they have to geo locate them on  this map and this is difficult because  these are not geo tagged tweets you have  to sort of do some sleuthing to figure  out like where this is and then they are  putting it there then creating this map  and they're doing all this in the course  of an hour right so this is a you know  this sort of complex ambiguous exercise  they're not given a lot of training in  it in advance and so they're also trying  to figure out how to do it so there's a  lot of chatting going on here so we're  really sort of studying this very rich  team collaborative exercise with a with  a with a relatively claimed performance  outcome which is how good a job did I do  of creating this map and globally they  do pretty well right so this is the  actual map which was produced by you  know 32 volunteers over 12 hours in 2012  and this is the map that our experiment  subjects produced in one hour a couple  of years later and they're actually  quite similar the difference is that we  are able to measure everything so we can  we can see you know every action that  everyone takes everything that everybody  says to somebody else and so we can look  at communication we can look at division  of labor and we can look at performance  and we can do this  for a large number of groups of  different sizes and we  can for each one of them the maps we can  we can construct a fairly clean measure  of quality how well they are able to to  identify the events that matter and so  the sort of short version is that if you  if you look at how the performance  varies with the size of the team it's  true that teams larger teams do better  than smaller teams so that seems  reasonable but it's also clear that  there's this sort of strong diminishing  returns to scale right that larger teams  are not doing proportionally better than  individuals and so maybe you think well  you know and so you can ask why that is  and the the answer is that people tend  to slack off when they're around other  people so this is a phenomenon called  that psychologists call social loafing  economists call it free writing so all  this sounds very plausible but then  something else turns up which is that in  larger teams there's a this other  benefit that you get from being in a  team which is that you know people are  kind of are checking each other's work  people are realizing that they're both  working on the same problem and  eliminating redundancies and so even  though there's a sort of tension here  where you find that you know when there  are other people around you're less  likely to work as hard but when there  are other people around you get some  sort of benefit as well and so you want  to try to to balance up these two  competing forces well it's unclear which  one is is more salient and so we have a  way of doing that by creating synthetic  groups out of individual workers so we  have all these people who are doing this  on their own and we can combine their  maps to create you know what they would  have done if we had treated them as a  team and so we find this very kind of  interesting and I think non obvious  result which is that if we compare the  synthetic teams to the real teams the  blue line is the synthetic teams the  real the green  as the real teams that first up to a  certain critical size the synthetic  teams are doing better so the the sort  of per capita effort is winning for a  while but then as the team size  increases the the the error checking and  and deduping that happens in the real  teams starts to win out and so the  largest team levels largest team sizes  you find that the teams are actually  better than individuals and  interestingly the red line shows you the  performance of the actual stand by task  force so as a sort of measure of  external validity we're running an  experiment here that is almost exactly I  mean it's not exactly like real-life but  it's very close to real life and so this  is you know something that is  tremendously exciting to us because you  know again if you go back through the  history of lab experiments they tend to  be extremely artificial and so you can  conduct these sort of nice clean  experiments but then you have to kind of  wave your hand about how the result  plays out in real life where lots of  other things are going on as well and  it's much Messier and so if we want to  understand how real teams and real  organizations perform and what is draw  you know which which factors drive  performance we have to be able to to to  strike a balance between lab light  control and and and naturalism and so  this is sort of getting much closer to  that so I think we are out of time but I  just want to say that you know sort of  end on a note of caution that that you  know this has been an extremely exciting  past decade there are things that we can  do now that were were quite unimaginable  to us just a decade ago in terms of the  the questions that we can answer the the  data that we can collect so there's  really enormous excitement there's new  conferences there's new research centres  there's new journals that new funding  agencies so there's a lot of interest in  computational approaches to social  science but there are some very large  challenges like in some sense you know  we are not any closer than we were a 10  or 15 years ago to answering the big  questions of social science that kind of  got all of us into this profession to  begin with and so going forward I think  we have to ask ourselves some some  serious questions about how we're going  to get the data that is the right data  so it's great to study retweets on  Twitter but in some sense you know we  don't really care about Twitter we care  about you know how does health behavior  spread through you know through the  population you know how do how do you  know cultural beliefs change like these  are the things that matter to the world  and these are the things that matter to  social science and you can kind of  squint your eyes and imagine that that  what we're studying on Twitter is sort  of like there's other stuff that we  really care about but at the end of the  day we have to really study the thing  that we care about and that's going to  require some some some serious  conversations between us and also  between US and industry in terms of you  know what we can measure how we can  build the instruments that are most  relevant to us and and and how we can  take what is sort of intriguing and  thought-provoking and and and turn it  into something that is that is  consequential for business and also for  society so on that note thanks very much  [Applause]
 

